{"step_index":72,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-10T10:42:07Z","input_tokens":3834,"cache_read_tokens":192076,"output_tokens":1100,"content":"I fixed all the issues shown in your terminal output and screenshot:\n\n### 1. Fixed the 5 `class Solution` Unit Test Failures in [engine.py](file:///C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/engine.py)\n- **Root Cause**: When Python executes `class Solution:` inside a restricted `__builtins__` dictionary, it internally calls `builtins.__build_class__`. Because `__build_class__` was missing from `SUPPORTED_BUILTINS`, `class Solution:` raised `NameError: name '__build_class__' is not defined`.\n- **Fix**: Added `\"__build_class__\": builtins.__build_class__`, `\"object\": object`, and standard exception classes (`IndexError`, `ValueError`, `TypeError`, `ZeroDivisionError`) to [`SUPPORTED_BUILTINS`](file:///C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/engine.py#L14-L42) in [engine.py](file:///C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/engine.py).\n\n### 2. Fixed the `/favicon.ico` 404 Log in [server.py](file:///C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/server.py) & [index.html](file:///C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/index.html)\n- Added a `do_GET` handler in [`VisualizerRequestHandler.do_GET`](file:///C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/server.py#L28-L33) that returns `204 No Content` for `/favicon.ico`, and added `<link rel=\"icon\" href=\"data:,\">` to [index.html](file:///C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/index.html#L7).\n\n### 3. UI Polish from Your Screenshot ([app.js](file:///C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/app.js) & [styles.css](file:///C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/styles.css))\n- Fixed the top padding of the SVG Flowchart in **Panel B** so the `START` and `class Solution` nodes at the top are never clipped.\n- Added an interactive **Execution Timeline Step Bar** (`#1 L1`, `#2 L2`, `#3 L2`...) right above the flowchart in **Panel B** ([`renderTimelineStrip`](file:///C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/app.js#L556-L581)) so you can click any of the 50 steps to jump directly to it.\n\n---\n\n### Verify Now\nRun the unit tests again in PowerShell:\n```powershell\npython -m unittest test_engine.py -v\n```\nAnd refresh **http://127.0.0.1:8080** (or press `Ctrl + F5`) in your browser to see the updated timeline strip and flowchart!","thinking":"The `class Solution` unit tests failed because the Python `class` statement calls `__build_class__` from `__builtins__`. This fundamental mechanism within Python was not properly handled. The failure stems from this core functionality not being present or accessible, leading to the reported errors.\n\n\n"}
{"step_index":73,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-10T10:51:55Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nYes, I agree. The current interface shows too much information at once. For a beginner, it feels like a developer debugging tool instead of a personal coding teacher.\r\n\r\nMy recommendation: simplify it into a “Teach Me This Code” interface.\r\n\r\nGive your AI coding tool this prompt to modify the existing application.\r\n\r\n1. Simplify the Interface\r\n\r\nRedesign my existing Code Flow Visualizer to make it extremely easy for a complete beginner to understand Python code.\r\n\r\nIMPORTANT: Do not rebuild the application from scratch. Modify the existing application and preserve its working execution engine. No AI APIs or external AI services.\r\n\r\n## 1. Simplify the Interface\r\n\r\nRemove the current complicated three-panel developer dashboard, large flowchart, excessive technical labels, and multiple information cards.\r\n\r\nUse a clean, beginner-friendly interface with a light background, large readable text, and minimal distractions.\r\n\r\nThe interface should have only three main areas:\r\n\r\n1. Python code.\r\n\r\n2. Step-by-step explanation.\r\n\r\n3. Variable values.\r\n\r\n## 2. Code Editor\r\n\r\nShow the Python code on the left.\r\n\r\nHighlight only the current line. Keep the remaining code visible but slightly less prominent.\r\n\r\nAdd a simple heading: \"Your Code\".\r\n\r\n## 3. Teaching Area\r\n\r\nMake this the main focus of the screen.\r\n\r\nShow one step at a time using this format:\r\n\r\n**Step 1: Create the variable**\r\n\r\nCode:\r\n`c = 0`\r\n\r\n**What does this mean?**\r\n\r\nWe create a variable named `c` and store the number `0` inside it.\r\n\r\n**Variable values now:**\r\n\r\n`c = 0`\r\n\r\n**What happens next?**\r\n\r\nPython moves to the next line and creates the variable `ans`.\r\n\r\nUse simple English. Explain programming concepts only when needed. Do not overwhelm the beginner with technical details.\r\n\r\n## 4. Variable Inspector\r\n\r\nDisplay only the variables relevant to the current step.\r\n\r\nFor example:\r\n\r\n| Variable | Current value |\r\n| -------- | ------------- |\r\n| c        | 0             |\r\n| ans      | -2            |\r\n\r\nHighlight a variable only when its value changes.\r\n\r\nHide irrelevant metadata, data types, global scope, execution event types, and technical tracing details from the beginner view.\r\n\r\n## 5. Simple Navigation\r\n\r\nAdd only these primary controls:\r\n\r\n* Previous\r\n\r\n* Next Step\r\n\r\n* Auto Play\r\n\r\n* Restart\r\n\r\nShow a simple progress label such as \"Step 4 of 20\".\r\n\r\nThe Next Step button should be prominent.\r\n\r\nWhen I click Next Step, explain the next meaningful operation and update the variable values.\r\n\r\nWhen I click Previous, restore the previous step and its variable values correctly.\r\n\r\n## 6. Explain the Example Like a Teacher\r\n\r\nUse this existing code:\r\n\r\n`class Solution: def maxSubArray(self, nums: list[int]) -> int: c = 0 ans = nums[0] for i in range(len(nums)): c += nums[i] if c >= ans: ans = c if c < 0: c = 0 return ans`\r\n\r\nUse the input:\r\n\r\n`[-2, 1, -3, 4, -1, 2, 1, -5, 4]`\r\n\r\nStart with a simple overview:\r\n\r\n\"This code finds the largest sum you can get from a continuous section of the list.\"\r\n\r\nExplain the class and function definitions briefly, then show the actual function call and begin execution.\r\n\r\nExplain each meaningful operation in order.\r\n\r\nFor example:\r\n\r\n**Step: Add the current number**\r\n\r\nCurrent number: `-2`\r\n\r\nBefore:\r\n`c = 0`\r\n\r\nCalculation:\r\n`c = 0 + (-2)`\r\n\r\nAfter:\r\n`c = -2`\r\n\r\nExplanation:\r\n\"We added -2 to c. Because the result is negative, the next condition will reset c to zero.\"\r\n\r\nThen explain the next condition separately.\r\n\r\nContinue through every iteration without skipping operations.\r\n\r\nAt the end, show:\r\n\r\n**Final answer: 6**\r\n\r\n\"The continuous section \\(4, -1, 2, 1\\) has the largest sum: 6.\"\r\n\r\n## 7. Beginner-Friendly Rules\r\n\r\n* Show one concept at a time.\r\n\r\n* Use simple English, not developer jargon.\r\n\r\n* Avoid showing everything on one screen.\r\n\r\n* Do not show a large flowchart by default.\r\n\r\n* Do not display unnecessary technical metadata.\r\n\r\n* Keep the real execution engine and actual variable values.\r\n\r\n* Do not fake execution results.\r\n\r\n* Do not remove Previous Step functionality.\r\n\r\n* Keep the application usable on a normal laptop.\r\n\r\n* Keep advanced debugging details hidden behind an optional \"Advanced View\" button.\r\n\r\n## Final Goal\r\n\r\nMake the interface feel like a personal teacher sitting beside me, explaining one line at a time.\r\n\r\nWhen I open the application, I should immediately understand where to look, what the current step means, and which button to click next.\r\n\r\nPriori